C({id:"neutron",name:"Discovery of the Neutron",type:"experiment",year:1932,era:"nuclear",fields:["nuclear"],people:["rutherford"],deps:["gold-foil"],
simple:"The nucleus contains not only protons but also neutral particles of nearly the same mass — neutrons.",
intuitive:"Nuclei weighed more than their protons could account for. Rutherford had predicted a neutral particle in 1920. Experiments by Bothe and Becker, then Irène and Frédéric Joliot-Curie, produced a penetrating neutral radiation from beryllium, which they took for gamma rays. James Chadwick (1932) showed it was a neutral particle about as heavy as the proton. Neutral, it could enter nuclei without being repelled — the perfect tool to probe and split them.",
formal:"$m_n \\approx 939.57$ MeV/c², slightly heavier than the proton (938.27). Free neutrons decay: $n \\to p + e^- + \\bar\\nu_e$, half-life ≈ 10 minutes.",
philosophy:"With protons, neutrons and electrons, matter seemed explained by three particles — a simplicity that lasted only briefly.",
why:"Neutrons trigger fission and are essential to nuclear reactors; neutron scattering probes materials.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Nuclear reactors; neutron radiography; neutron scattering for materials."},{to:"science",text:"Isotopes explained; nuclear physics."}]});
C({id:"neutrino",name:"The Neutrino & Beta Decay",type:"theory",year:1930,era:"nuclear",fields:["nuclear","particle"],people:["pauli","fermi"],deps:["energy-conservation","radioactivity"],
simple:"In beta decay some energy seemed to vanish — so Pauli proposed an invisible, almost massless particle carrying it away.",
intuitive:"In beta decay a nucleus emits an electron, but the electrons came out with a range of energies, not one fixed value. Energy conservation seemed violated; Bohr even considered abandoning it. Pauli (letter of December 1930, addressed to 'Dear radioactive ladies and gentlemen') proposed a neutral, very light particle sharing the energy. Fermi named it 'neutrino' (little neutral one) and built the theory of beta decay (1934) — the first theory of the weak force. Cowan and Reines detected neutrinos from a nuclear reactor in 1956.",
formal:"$n \\to p + e^- + \\bar\\nu_e$. Fermi's four-fermion contact interaction with coupling $G_F \\approx 1.166\\times10^{-5}\\ \\text{GeV}^{-2}$.",
philosophy:"Faith in a conservation law led to predicting an undetectable particle — later detected. Neutrinos pass through Earth almost unhindered: trillions pass through your body every second.",
why:"The discovery of neutrino oscillations (Super-Kamiokande 1998, SNO 2001) proved neutrinos have mass — physics beyond the original Standard Model.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Solar neutrinos confirm fusion in the Sun's core; supernova 1987A neutrinos; neutrino astronomy."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Trust in conservation laws as a guide."}]});
C({id:"yukawa-meson",name:"Yukawa's Meson & Force Carriers",type:"theory",year:1935,era:"nuclear",fields:["nuclear","particle","qft"],people:["yukawa"],deps:["neutron","uncertainty"],
simple:"Forces are carried by exchanged particles; the short reach of the nuclear force means its carrier must be heavy.",
intuitive:"What holds protons together against their electric repulsion? Yukawa (1935) proposed they exchange a particle. Using the uncertainty principle, a heavy particle can exist only briefly and therefore travel only a short distance — explaining why the nuclear force acts only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m. He predicted a mass about 200 times the electron's. The pion was found in cosmic rays in 1947 (Powell, Occhialini, Lattes).",
formal:"Range $R \\approx \\dfrac{\\hbar}{mc}$; Yukawa potential $$V(r) = -g^2\\frac{e^{-r/R}}{r}.$$ With $R \\approx 1.4$ fm, $mc^2 \\approx 140$ MeV.",
philosophy:"The exchange picture made 'force' into a particle process. In the modern Standard Model, the nuclear force is a residual effect of the strong force between quarks, carried by gluons.",
why:"The template for understanding all forces as exchanges of bosons.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Particle exchange became the language of the Standard Model."}],
note:"A muon found in 1936–37 was first mistaken for Yukawa's particle."});
C({id:"fission",name:"Nuclear Fission",type:"experiment",year:1938,era:"nuclear",fields:["nuclear"],people:["meitner","fermi"],deps:["neutron","mass-energy"],
simple:"A uranium nucleus hit by a neutron can split in two, releasing enormous energy and more neutrons.",
intuitive:"In December 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann in Berlin found barium — about half uranium's mass — after bombarding uranium with neutrons. Lise Meitner, exiled in Sweden, and her nephew Otto Frisch interpreted it over Christmas: the nucleus, like a wobbling liquid drop, stretches and splits. The fragments weigh less than the original; the missing mass becomes about 200 MeV of energy by E = mc². Since each fission releases 2–3 neutrons, a chain reaction is possible.",
formal:"Example: $$^{235}\\text{U} + n \\to {}^{141}\\text{Ba} + {}^{92}\\text{Kr} + 3n + \\approx 200\\ \\text{MeV}.$$ One kilogram of U-235 fully fissioned ≈ 8×10¹³ J, about the energy of 2–3 million kg of coal.",
philosophy:"Discovered months before the Second World War, fission immediately raised the possibility of bombs. Scientists faced unprecedented moral choices.",
why:"Nuclear power (about 9–10% of world electricity) and nuclear weapons.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Nuclear power plants, naval propulsion, medical isotope production."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Responsibility of scientists; nuclear deterrence."}],
note:"Hahn alone received the 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry; Meitner's exclusion is widely regarded as an injustice. Ida Noddack had suggested in 1934 that nuclei might break into large fragments, but was largely ignored."});
C({id:"chain-reaction",name:"Chain Reaction & Chicago Pile-1",type:"experiment",year:1942,era:"nuclear",fields:["nuclear"],people:["fermi"],deps:["fission"],
simple:"Neutrons from one fission trigger more fissions — controlled, it powers a reactor; uncontrolled, a bomb.",
intuitive:"Leo Szilard conceived a neutron chain reaction in 1933. If, on average, exactly one neutron from each fission causes another, the reaction is steady (critical). Fermi's team built Chicago Pile-1 under the stands of a university sports field: a pile of graphite blocks (to slow neutrons) with uranium, controlled by cadmium rods that absorb neutrons. On 2 December 1942 it became the first human-made self-sustaining chain reaction.",
formal:"Multiplication factor $k$: subcritical $k<1$, critical $k=1$, supercritical $k>1$. Delayed neutrons (~0.65% for U-235) make reactors controllable.",
philosophy:"Physics moved from small laboratories to industrial-scale, state-run 'big science' (the Manhattan Project employed over 100,000 people).",
why:"The beginning of nuclear engineering.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Reactor design, control rods, moderators; nuclear safety engineering."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Big science and its political entanglements."}]});
C({id:"qed",name:"Quantum Electrodynamics (QED) & Feynman Diagrams",type:"theory",year:1948,era:"nuclear",fields:["qft","particle"],people:["feynman","dirac"],deps:["dirac-equation","field-concept","least-action"],
simple:"The quantum theory of light and electrons — the most precisely tested theory in science, calculated with simple pictures.",
intuitive:"Early attempts at quantum field theory gave infinite answers. After WWII, precise measurements (the Lamb shift, the electron's magnetic moment) demanded a real theory. Tomonaga, Schwinger and Feynman independently found how to tame the infinities by 'renormalization' — absorbing them into measured mass and charge; Dyson showed the approaches were equivalent. Feynman's diagrams turned calculations into drawings: lines for particles, wiggles for photons, vertices for interactions. Each diagram is a term in a sum.",
formal:"Fine-structure constant $\\alpha = \\dfrac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\hbar c} \\approx \\dfrac{1}{137.036}$. Electron anomalous magnetic moment: theory and experiment agree to better than one part in a billion.",
philosophy:"Renormalization initially looked like sweeping infinities under the rug (Dirac disliked it). Kenneth Wilson (1970s) reinterpreted it: physics at each scale is described by an effective theory insensitive to unknown details at smaller scales.",
why:"Explains all of chemistry and light–matter interaction in principle; the model for the rest of the Standard Model.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Precision atomic physics; template for electroweak theory and QCD."},{to:"technology",text:"Laser physics and quantum optics rely on QED concepts."}]});
C({id:"parity-violation",name:"Parity Violation (Wu Experiment)",type:"experiment",year:1957,era:"nuclear",fields:["particle","nuclear"],people:["wu"],deps:["neutrino","noether-theorem"],
simple:"The weak force distinguishes left from right — nature is not mirror-symmetric.",
intuitive:"Everyone assumed the laws of physics look the same in a mirror (parity symmetry). Tsung-Dao Lee and Chen-Ning Yang (1956) noticed this had never been tested for the weak force. Chien-Shiung Wu and colleagues at the National Bureau of Standards cooled cobalt-60 to about 0.01 K and aligned the nuclear spins with a magnetic field. The beta-decay electrons came out preferentially opposite to the spin direction. In the mirror image they would go the other way — so the mirror world behaves differently. Pauli had bet against it.",
formal:"Under parity P: $\\mathbf r \\to -\\mathbf r$. Measured asymmetry in $\\langle\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf p_e\\rangle \\ne 0$, which is P-odd. The weak interaction couples only to left-handed particles (V−A theory).",
philosophy:"A symmetry considered self-evident turned out false — a lesson in testing assumptions. Later CP violation (1964) showed that even matter–antimatter combined mirror symmetry fails slightly, relevant to why matter dominates.",
why:"Shaped the structure of the electroweak theory.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"V−A theory; CP violation and matter–antimatter asymmetry research."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Nature has a handedness at the fundamental level."}]});
C({id:"quarks",name:"Quarks & Quantum Chromodynamics",type:"theory",year:1964,era:"nuclear",fields:["particle","qft"],people:["gellmann"],deps:["yukawa-meson","gauge-symmetry"],math:[["group","Group theory"]],
simple:"Protons and neutrons are made of smaller particles, quarks, held together by gluons of the strong force.",
intuitive:"By the 1960s accelerators had found hundreds of 'elementary' particles. Gell-Mann and Ne'eman organised them into symmetry patterns (the Eightfold Way, 1961), like a periodic table. Gell-Mann and Zweig (1964) proposed the pattern came from three building blocks — up, down, strange quarks — with fractional charges. Electron scattering at SLAC (1968) saw point-like constituents inside protons. QCD (1973) says quarks carry 'colour' charge exchanged by gluons. Unlike other forces, the strong force grows with distance, so quarks are never seen alone (confinement), but becomes weak at short distances (asymptotic freedom: Gross, Wilczek, Politzer).",
formal:"Proton = uud, neutron = udd; charges $u = +\\tfrac23e$, $d = -\\tfrac13e$. QCD is an SU(3) gauge theory. Six quark flavours: u, d, s, c, b, t (top found 1995).",
philosophy:"Particles that can never be isolated — are they 'real'? The consensus says yes, given overwhelming indirect evidence. Most of the mass of ordinary matter comes from the energy of quarks and gluons in motion, not from the quarks' own masses.",
why:"Completes the picture of nuclear matter; the strong-force half of the Standard Model.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Structure of protons and neutrons; quark–gluon plasma of the early universe."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Confinement and the reality of unobservable entities."}]});
C({id:"gauge-symmetry",name:"Gauge Symmetry (Yang–Mills)",type:"concept",year:1954,era:"nuclear",fields:["qft","particle"],people:["noether"],deps:["noether-theorem","qed"],math:[["group","Group theory"],["manifold","Manifolds"]],
simple:"Demanding that certain symmetries hold independently at every point of space forces forces to exist.",
intuitive:"In QED, the electron's wavefunction has a phase you can't observe. If you insist you may change that phase differently at every point (a local symmetry), the equations only stay consistent if you add a field that compensates — and that field is exactly the electromagnetic field, with the photon as its particle. Yang and Mills (1954) generalised this to more complex symmetries; these became the weak and strong forces. Forces are, in this sense, consequences of symmetry.",
formal:"Standard Model gauge group: $$SU(3)_C \\times SU(2)_L \\times U(1)_Y.$$ Covariant derivative $D_\\mu = \\partial_\\mu - igA_\\mu^aT^a$.",
philosophy:"Perhaps the deepest idea in modern physics: the forces of nature are dictated by symmetry. The 'gauge' word comes from Hermann Weyl's 1918–1929 work on scale (gauge) invariance.",
why:"The organising principle of the entire Standard Model.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"All three non-gravitational forces are gauge theories."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Symmetry as the source of interaction."}]});
C({id:"higgs-mechanism",name:"Higgs Mechanism",type:"theory",year:1964,era:"nuclear",fields:["particle","qft"],people:["higgs"],deps:["gauge-symmetry","phase-transitions"],
simple:"A field filling all space gives particles their mass; its ripples are the Higgs boson.",
intuitive:"Gauge symmetry seemed to require force carriers to be massless, but the weak force's carriers must be heavy (it is so short-range). In 1964 several groups showed a way out: a field with nonzero value everywhere in the vacuum 'hides' the symmetry (spontaneous symmetry breaking, as in a magnet choosing a direction). Particles interacting with this field behave as if massive. One popular analogy is a celebrity slowed by a crowd at a party; it captures the idea of interaction but not the details.",
formal:"Potential $V(\\phi) = -\\mu^2|\\phi|^2 + \\lambda|\\phi|^4$ has its minimum at $|\\phi| = v/\\sqrt2$, with $v \\approx 246$ GeV. W and Z masses $m_W = \\tfrac12 g v$; fermion masses $m_f = y_f v/\\sqrt2$.",
philosophy:"Mass is not an intrinsic property but a result of interaction with the vacuum. The vacuum itself has structure.",
why:"Makes the electroweak theory consistent and predicts the Higgs boson (found 2012).",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Masses of W, Z, quarks and leptons; possible link to cosmology (vacuum stability, inflation)."},{to:"philosophy",text:"The vacuum is not nothing."}],
note:"Found independently by Englert & Brout, Higgs, and Guralnik, Hagen & Kibble (1964), building on ideas of Nambu, Goldstone and Anderson. The Nobel (2013) went to Englert and Higgs."});
C({id:"electroweak",name:"Electroweak Unification",type:"theory",year:1967,era:"nuclear",fields:["particle","qft"],people:["glashow","salam","weinberg"],deps:["gauge-symmetry","higgs-mechanism","neutrino","parity-violation"],
simple:"Electromagnetism and the weak nuclear force are two faces of a single force at high energies.",
intuitive:"Glashow (1961) proposed the gauge structure; Weinberg (1967) and Salam (1968) added the Higgs mechanism. The theory predicted 'neutral currents' (found at CERN, 1973) and the W and Z bosons with specific masses (found at CERN, 1983, by Rubbia's and van der Meer's teams). At everyday energies the Higgs field makes W and Z heavy, so the weak force is weak and short-range while the photon stays massless.",
formal:"$SU(2)_L\\times U(1)_Y \\to U(1)_{\\text{EM}}$. Weinberg angle relates couplings: $e = g\\sin\\theta_W$; $m_W \\approx 80.4$ GeV, $m_Z \\approx 91.2$ GeV.",
philosophy:"After Newton (heaven and earth) and Maxwell (electricity and magnetism), the next great unification. 't Hooft and Veltman proved it renormalizable (1971), making it a real predictive theory.",
why:"Core of the Standard Model.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Standard Model; motivation for grand unification."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Unification as the driving ideal of physics."}]});
C({id:"standard-model",name:"The Standard Model of Particle Physics",type:"theory",year:1975,era:"nuclear",fields:["particle","qft"],people:["gellmann","weinberg","glashow","salam","higgs","feynman"],deps:["electroweak","quarks","qed"],
simple:"The theory listing all known elementary particles and three of the four forces — astonishingly accurate, but known to be incomplete.",
intuitive:"Matter: six quarks and six leptons (electron, muon, tau and three neutrinos), in three 'generations'. Forces: the photon (electromagnetism), W and Z (weak), eight gluons (strong). Plus the Higgs boson. It was assembled in the early-to-mid 1970s and has passed every accelerator test since. What it leaves out: gravity, dark matter, dark energy, neutrino masses (in its original form), and why matter outnumbers antimatter. It also has about 19 free parameters measured but not explained.",
formal:"$$\\mathcal L_{SM} = -\\tfrac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu} + i\\bar\\psi\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu\\psi + |D_\\mu\\phi|^2 - V(\\phi) + (y_{ij}\\bar\\psi_i\\phi\\psi_j + \\text{h.c.})$$ — compact notation for gauge fields, fermions, Higgs and Yukawa couplings.",
philosophy:"The most precise theory ever made, yet 'unnatural' and incomplete. Many physicists see it as an effective theory — a low-energy approximation of something deeper.",
why:"Summarises 20th-century particle physics.",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Accelerator, detector and computing technologies (the Web was created at CERN in 1989)."},{to:"science",text:"Cosmology of the early universe."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Reductionism's greatest triumph and its limits."}]});
C({id:"laser",name:"Stimulated Emission & the Laser",type:"theory",year:1917,era:"nuclear",fields:["quantum","optics","atomic"],people:["einstein"],deps:["bohr-atom","blackbody"],
simple:"An incoming photon can trigger an excited atom to emit an identical photon; chain this and you get a laser.",
intuitive:"In 1917 Einstein showed that to get Planck's law right, atoms must not only absorb and spontaneously emit light, but also emit when 'stimulated' by a photon of the right frequency — producing a twin photon with the same direction and phase. If more atoms are excited than not (population inversion), light gets amplified. Masers (Townes, 1954) and lasers (Maiman's ruby laser, 1960) followed, after Basov, Prokhorov, Townes and Schawlow laid the theory.",
formal:"Einstein coefficients $A_{21}$, $B_{21}$, $B_{12}$ with $\\dfrac{A_{21}}{B_{21}} = \\dfrac{8\\pi h\\nu^3}{c^3}$. Gain requires $N_2 > N_1$ (population inversion).",
philosophy:"A 43-year gap from theory to device. When built, the laser was called 'a solution looking for a problem'; it now underpins much of modern technology.",
why:"Lasers are everywhere: communications, manufacturing, medicine, measurement.",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Fibre-optic internet, barcode scanners, optical storage, lidar, laser printers."},{to:"engineering",text:"Laser cutting and welding, surveying, interferometry, LIGO."},{to:"science",text:"Laser surgery, spectroscopy, atomic cooling."}],
note:"Gordon Gould coined 'laser' and fought a decades-long patent battle."});
C({id:"stellar-fusion",name:"How Stars Shine: Nuclear Fusion",type:"theory",year:1939,era:"cosmos",fields:["astrophysics","nuclear"],people:["chandrasekhar"],deps:["mass-energy","tunneling"],
simple:"Stars shine by fusing hydrogen into helium in their cores, turning a little mass into energy.",
intuitive:"In the 19th century, Kelvin and Helmholtz estimated the Sun could shine by gravitational contraction for only tens of millions of years — too short for geology and evolution. Eddington (1920) suggested converting hydrogen into helium releases energy (helium weighs 0.7% less than four hydrogens). Quantum tunneling lets protons overcome their repulsion at the Sun's 15-million-degree core. Hans Bethe (1939) worked out the reaction chains (proton–proton chain and CNO cycle). Burbidge, Burbidge, Fowler and Hoyle (1957) showed how stars forge heavier elements.",
formal:"Net: $$4\\,{}^1\\text{H} \\to {}^4\\text{He} + 2e^+ + 2\\nu_e + 26.7\\ \\text{MeV}.$$ The Sun converts about 4 million tonnes of mass to energy per second.",
philosophy:"We are made of star stuff: the carbon, oxygen and iron in our bodies were made in stars and scattered by supernovae.",
why:"Explains the lifetime of stars and the origin of the elements; motivates fusion energy research.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Fusion reactor research (tokamaks such as ITER, inertial confinement — ignition achieved at NIF in 2022)."},{to:"science",text:"Nucleosynthesis; stellar evolution."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Cosmic origin of the elements of life."}]});
C({id:"expanding-universe",name:"The Expanding Universe (Hubble–Lemaître Law)",type:"law",year:1929,era:"cosmos",fields:["cosmology"],people:["lemaitre","hubble"],deps:["general-relativity","doppler"],
simple:"Distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther they are, the faster — space itself is expanding.",
intuitive:"Einstein (1917) added a cosmological constant to keep his universe static. Friedmann (1922) and Lemaître (1927) found expanding solutions. Slipher measured redshifts of spiral nebulae; Hubble, with Humason, established distances using Cepheid stars (calibrated by Leavitt's law). The data showed recession speed proportional to distance. Picture raisins in rising bread: every raisin sees all others receding, faster for those farther away — there is no centre.",
formal:"$$v = H_0\\, d,\\qquad H_0 \\approx 67\\text{–}73\\ \\text{km/s/Mpc}.$$ The 'Hubble tension' — different methods giving ~67 vs ~73 — is an active research puzzle. $1/H_0$ gives a rough age scale (~14 billion years).",
philosophy:"The universe is not eternal and static, but evolving. Einstein reportedly later called the cosmological constant his biggest blunder (reported by Gamow; the quote is uncertain). Ironically, the constant returned in 1998 as dark energy.",
why:"The observational foundation of Big Bang cosmology.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Cosmology; age of the universe."},{to:"philosophy",text:"A universe with a history and possibly a beginning."}],
note:"The IAU (2018) recommended the name Hubble–Lemaître law to credit Lemaître's 1927 derivation and estimate."});
C({id:"big-bang",name:"The Big Bang Theory",type:"theory",year:1931,era:"cosmos",fields:["cosmology"],people:["lemaitre"],deps:["expanding-universe"],
simple:"The universe began about 13.8 billion years ago in an extremely hot, dense state and has been expanding and cooling ever since.",
intuitive:"Run the expansion backward and everything converges into a hot, dense early state. Lemaître called it the 'primeval atom' (1931). Gamow, Alpher and Herman (1948) calculated that the first minutes would produce helium and deuterium in definite proportions and leave a relic radiation. Observed: about 25% of ordinary matter by mass is helium, as predicted, and the relic radiation was found in 1965. The theory describes the evolution from a fraction of a second onward; it does not say what (if anything) came 'before'.",
formal:"Friedmann equation: $$\\left(\\frac{\\dot a}{a}\\right)^2 = \\frac{8\\pi G}{3}\\rho - \\frac{kc^2}{a^2} + \\frac{\\Lambda c^2}{3}.$$ Age (Planck 2018): 13.79 ± 0.02 billion years in the ΛCDM model.",
philosophy:"Not an explosion in space but an expansion of space. Whether t = 0 marks a true beginning is an open question requiring quantum gravity. Fred Hoyle, a proponent of the rival steady-state theory, coined the name in 1949.",
why:"The standard model of cosmology, supported by expansion, the CMB, light-element abundances and large-scale structure.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Origin of light elements; framework of all cosmology."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Creation, beginnings, and the limits of science."}]});
C({id:"cmb",name:"Cosmic Microwave Background",type:"experiment",year:1965,era:"cosmos",fields:["cosmology"],people:[],deps:["big-bang","blackbody"],
simple:"The faint microwave glow left over from the hot early universe fills the whole sky.",
intuitive:"About 380,000 years after the Big Bang the universe cooled enough for atoms to form, and light could travel freely. That light, stretched by expansion, now arrives as microwaves at 2.725 K from every direction. Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson at Bell Labs (1965) found an unexplained hiss in their horn antenna (they even cleaned out pigeon droppings); nearby, Dicke's group at Princeton was preparing to look for it. COBE (1992) showed a perfect blackbody spectrum and tiny ripples; WMAP and Planck mapped them in detail.",
formal:"Blackbody at $T = 2.72548 \\pm 0.00057$ K; fluctuations $\\Delta T/T \\sim 10^{-5}$. Its angular power spectrum fixes cosmological parameters: ~5% ordinary matter, ~27% dark matter, ~68% dark energy.",
philosophy:"A baby picture of the universe. An accidental discovery by radio engineers decided between rival cosmologies — engineering noise became cosmic evidence.",
why:"The strongest evidence for the Big Bang and a precision tool for cosmology.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Low-noise microwave receivers and cryogenic detectors."},{to:"science",text:"Precision cosmology."}],
note:"Some of the 'snow' on old analogue TVs between channels was caused by the CMB (roughly 1%)."});
C({id:"chandrasekhar-limit",name:"Chandrasekhar Limit",type:"law",year:1930,era:"cosmos",fields:["astrophysics"],people:["chandrasekhar"],deps:["pauli-exclusion","special-relativity"],
simple:"A dead star held up only by electron pressure cannot exceed about 1.4 times the Sun's mass; heavier ones collapse further.",
intuitive:"When a star like the Sun runs out of fuel, its core shrinks into a white dwarf — Earth-sized, held up by electron degeneracy pressure (the exclusion principle forbids electrons from crowding further). Chandrasekhar (1930) included relativity: in heavier dwarfs, electrons move near light speed and the pressure can't keep up with gravity. Above the limit, no stable white dwarf exists: the core collapses to a neutron star or black hole.",
formal:"$$M_{\\text{Ch}} \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt{3\\pi}}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\hbar c}{G}\\right)^{3/2}\\frac{1}{(\\mu_e m_H)^2} \\approx 1.4\\,M_\\odot\\ \\ (\\mu_e = 2).$$",
philosophy:"A quantum effect (exclusion) combined with relativity and gravity sets the fate of stars — a beautiful unity of physics. Eddington refused to accept that nature would allow such collapse.",
why:"Explains Type Ia supernovae, used as 'standard candles' that revealed dark energy.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Stellar death, supernovae, neutron stars, black holes."}]});
C({id:"black-holes",name:"Black Holes",type:"concept",year:1916,era:"cosmos",fields:["general-relativity","astrophysics"],people:["einstein","penrose","hawking","chandrasekhar"],deps:["general-relativity"],demo:"curvature",
simple:"Regions where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape.",
intuitive:"John Michell (1783) and Laplace imagined 'dark stars' with escape speed above light's. Within weeks of Einstein's 1915 equations, Karl Schwarzschild (writing from the WWI Russian front) found an exact solution with a critical radius, the event horizon. Oppenheimer and Snyder (1939) showed massive stars could collapse into one. Wheeler popularized the name (1967). Evidence: X-ray binaries (Cygnus X-1, 1970s), stars orbiting the Milky Way's centre (Sgr A*, ~4 million solar masses), gravitational waves from merging black holes (2015), and the Event Horizon Telescope image of M87* (2019).",
formal:"Schwarzschild radius $$r_s = \\frac{2GM}{c^2}$$ (≈ 3 km per solar mass; Earth would be ≈ 9 mm). Time dilation near the horizon: $d\\tau = \\sqrt{1 - r_s/r}\\,dt$.",
philosophy:"The horizon is a one-way surface in spacetime. Inside, general relativity predicts a singularity — a sign that the theory breaks down and quantum gravity is needed.",
why:"Powerhouses of quasars, laboratories for strong gravity, and the key test bed for quantum gravity ideas.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Quasars, galaxy evolution, gravitational-wave sources."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Limits of knowledge, information and causality."}]});
C({id:"singularity-theorems",name:"Singularity Theorems",type:"theory",year:1965,era:"cosmos",fields:["general-relativity","cosmology"],people:["penrose","hawking"],deps:["black-holes","general-relativity"],math:[["manifold","Manifolds & topology"]],
simple:"Under very general conditions, general relativity predicts that collapse leads to singularities and that the universe began with one.",
intuitive:"Before 1965 many thought singularities were artefacts of perfectly symmetric idealized solutions; real, lumpy collapse might avoid them. Penrose (1965) introduced the 'trapped surface' — a region where even outgoing light is dragged inward — and proved, using topology rather than exact solutions, that once one forms, a singularity must follow. Hawking and Penrose (1970) extended this to cosmology: if general relativity holds and matter has positive energy, our universe had a singular beginning.",
formal:"If spacetime contains a trapped surface, satisfies the null energy condition $R_{\\mu\\nu}k^\\mu k^\\nu \\ge 0$, and is globally hyperbolic, then it is geodesically incomplete.",
philosophy:"General relativity predicts its own breakdown. Singularities signal where a deeper theory — quantum gravity — must take over.",
why:"Made black holes a robust prediction and transformed relativity with global geometric methods. Nobel Prize to Penrose, 2020.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Black-hole physics; the Big Bang singularity question."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Limits of a physical theory, foretold by itself."}]});
C({id:"hawking-radiation",name:"Hawking Radiation",type:"theory",year:1974,era:"cosmos",fields:["general-relativity","qft","frontiers"],people:["hawking"],deps:["black-holes","entropy"],
simple:"Quantum effects make black holes glow faintly and slowly evaporate.",
intuitive:"Jacob Bekenstein (1972) argued black holes must have entropy proportional to their horizon area. Hawking set out to refute it and instead found (1974) that, when quantum fields are included, black holes emit thermal radiation. A common picture: virtual particle pairs near the horizon, one falling in and one escaping — Hawking himself said this is heuristic. The temperature is tiny for stellar black holes (~60 nanokelvin for one solar mass), far colder than the CMB, so it has not been observed.",
formal:"$$T_H = \\frac{\\hbar c^3}{8\\pi G M k_B},\\qquad S_{BH} = \\frac{k_B c^3 A}{4G\\hbar}.$$ Evaporation time $\\propto M^3$ (~10⁶⁷ years for a solar-mass black hole).",
philosophy:"Gravity, quantum theory and thermodynamics meet in one formula containing G, ħ, c and k. It raises the information paradox: if a black hole evaporates into thermal radiation, is information about what fell in destroyed, violating quantum mechanics? Still debated.",
why:"The central clue for quantum gravity research.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Black-hole thermodynamics, holography, quantum-gravity research."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Information loss and determinism."}]});
C({id:"superconductivity",name:"Superconductivity",type:"experiment",year:1911,era:"complex",fields:["condensed-matter"],people:["onnes","bardeen","landau"],deps:["absolute-zero","pauli-exclusion"],
simple:"Below a critical temperature some materials conduct electricity with exactly zero resistance and expel magnetic fields.",
intuitive:"Kamerlingh Onnes (1911) found mercury's resistance vanished below 4.2 K. Currents in superconducting rings can flow for years without decaying. Meissner and Ochsenfeld (1933) found superconductors expel magnetic fields — magnets levitate above them. The explanation came 46 years after discovery: BCS theory (Bardeen, Cooper, Schrieffer, 1957). Electrons, normally repelling, pair up via vibrations of the crystal lattice into 'Cooper pairs', which condense into a single collective quantum state that cannot be scattered by small imperfections. High-temperature cuprate superconductors (Bednorz and Müller, 1986) work above liquid-nitrogen temperature (77 K) and are still not fully understood.",
formal:"Energy gap $\\Delta \\approx 1.76\\,k_BT_c$ (BCS). Flux quantization $\\Phi_0 = h/2e$ — the '2e' proves pairing. Josephson effect (1962).",
philosophy:"A macroscopic quantum phenomenon: a whole wire behaves like one quantum wave. Its symmetry breaking inspired the Higgs mechanism.",
why:"MRI magnets, particle accelerators, SQUID sensors, superconducting qubits.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"MRI scanners, LHC magnets, maglev demonstrations, fault-current limiters, SQUID magnetometers."},{to:"technology",text:"Superconducting quantum computers (transmon qubits)."}]});
C({id:"band-theory",name:"Band Theory of Solids",type:"theory",year:1928,era:"complex",fields:["condensed-matter","quantum"],people:["pauli"],deps:["schrodinger-equation","pauli-exclusion"],math:[["fourier-series","Fourier analysis"]],
simple:"In a crystal, electron energies form bands separated by gaps — whether a band is full or partly full decides if a material is a metal, semiconductor or insulator.",
intuitive:"Felix Bloch (1928) solved Schrödinger's equation for an electron in a periodic crystal: the waves spread through the whole lattice. Allowed energies group into bands with forbidden gaps between them (Peierls, Wilson, Brillouin). In a metal the top band is partly filled — electrons move freely. In an insulator it is completely full and the gap is large. A semiconductor has a small gap (~1 eV for silicon): heat, light or added impurities (doping) put carriers into the conduction band, or create 'holes' — missing electrons acting as positive charges.",
formal:"Bloch's theorem: $\\psi_{\\mathbf k}(\\mathbf r) = e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf r}u_{\\mathbf k}(\\mathbf r)$, $u$ periodic. Silicon band gap $E_g \\approx 1.12$ eV at 300 K.",
philosophy:"'Holes' — absences behaving like particles — are a striking example of emergent 'quasiparticles', real in their effects yet not fundamental.",
why:"The foundation of semiconductor electronics.",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Transistors, diodes, LEDs, solar cells, laser diodes, integrated circuits."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Quasiparticles and emergence."}]});
C({id:"transistor",name:"The Transistor",type:"experiment",year:1947,era:"complex",fields:["condensed-matter"],people:["bardeen"],deps:["band-theory"],
simple:"A small semiconductor switch/amplifier that replaced vacuum tubes and made the digital age possible.",
intuitive:"At Bell Labs, John Bardeen and Walter Brattain (December 1947) pressed two gold contacts onto germanium and found that a small current in one controlled a larger one in the other — amplification in a solid. William Shockley invented the more practical junction transistor (1948). The silicon integrated circuit (Kilby 1958, Noyce 1959) and MOSFET (Atalla and Kahng, 1959) followed. Today a chip holds tens of billions of transistors, each tens of atoms across in its smallest features.",
formal:"MOSFET: a gate voltage $V_G$ above threshold $V_T$ creates a conducting channel; in saturation $I_D \\approx \\tfrac12\\mu C_{ox}\\frac{W}{L}(V_G - V_T)^2$. Moore's observation (1965): transistor counts double roughly every two years.",
philosophy:"Quantum mechanics, the most abstract physics, became the most practical. The transistor may be the most manufactured object in human history.",
why:"All computers, phones, and digital electronics.",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Computers, smartphones, the internet, digital everything."},{to:"engineering",text:"Power electronics, control systems, sensors."},{to:"philosophy",text:"The information age and its social transformations."}],
note:"Julius Lilienfeld patented field-effect transistor concepts in the 1920s but could not build a working device with the materials of the time."});
C({id:"phase-transitions",name:"Phase Transitions & Symmetry Breaking",type:"concept",year:1937,era:"complex",fields:["condensed-matter","stat-mech"],people:["landau"],deps:["boltzmann-entropy"],
simple:"When water freezes or a magnet forms, matter suddenly reorganizes and loses some symmetry; near the critical point, very different systems behave identically.",
intuitive:"Liquid water looks the same in all directions; ice crystals have preferred directions — symmetry is broken. Heat a magnet past its Curie temperature (~770 °C for iron) and it loses magnetisation; cool it and the atomic magnets spontaneously align in some direction. Landau (1937) described transitions by an 'order parameter' that is zero in the symmetric phase and nonzero in the ordered one. Near critical points, fluctuations occur at all scales, and systems as different as fluids and magnets share the same 'critical exponents' — universality, explained by Kenneth Wilson's renormalization group (1971).",
formal:"Landau free energy: $F = F_0 + a(T - T_c)\\,m^2 + b\\,m^4$. Below $T_c$, the minimum shifts to $m \\neq 0$. Near $T_c$: $m \\propto (T_c - T)^\\beta$.",
philosophy:"Spontaneous symmetry breaking: the laws are symmetric but the state is not — like a pencil balanced on its tip that must fall some way. This idea crossed into particle physics (Higgs) and cosmology (early-universe transitions).",
why:"Materials engineering, magnets, superconductors, and the Higgs mechanism.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Metallurgy (heat treatment), magnetic materials, liquid crystals in displays."},{to:"science",text:"Universality; symmetry breaking in particle physics and cosmology."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Emergence: 'more is different' (Anderson)."}]});
C({id:"chaos",name:"Deterministic Chaos",type:"concept",year:1963,era:"complex",fields:["chaos"],people:["lorenz"],deps:["laplace-demon","hamiltonian"],demo:"doublependulum",math:[["butterfly-effect","Butterfly effect (math)"],["mandelbrot","Fractals"],["three-body","Three-body problem"]],
simple:"Simple systems obeying exact laws can still be unpredictable, because tiny differences in starting conditions grow exponentially.",
intuitive:"A double pendulum — just two rods — swings wildly; start two copies almost identically and within seconds they move completely differently. Poincaré (1890) found this in the three-body problem. Edward Lorenz (1963) rediscovered it when re-entering a weather simulation with numbers rounded from six to three decimals: the forecast diverged completely. His 1972 talk asked whether a butterfly's wing in Brazil could set off a tornado in Texas.",
formal:"Sensitive dependence: $|\\delta x(t)| \\approx |\\delta x(0)|\\,e^{\\lambda t}$ with Lyapunov exponent $\\lambda > 0$. Predictability horizon $\\sim \\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\ln\\frac{\\text{tolerance}}{\\text{initial error}}$. Lorenz system: $\\dot x = \\sigma(y-x),\\ \\dot y = x(\\rho - z) - y,\\ \\dot z = xy - \\beta z$.",
philosophy:"Determinism ≠ predictability. Laplace's demon would need infinite precision. Chaos also reveals hidden order: strange attractors with fractal structure, and universal routes to chaos (Feigenbaum constants).",
why:"Weather forecasting limits (~2 weeks), ensemble forecasting, and stability analysis in engineering.",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Nonlinear vibration analysis, mixing design, chaos control, secure communication ideas."},{to:"science",text:"Meteorology, ecology, cardiology, orbital dynamics of asteroids."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Limits of prediction in a deterministic world."}]});
C({id:"dark-matter",name:"Dark Matter",type:"open-question",year:1975,era:"frontier",fields:["cosmology","astrophysics","frontiers"],people:["rubin"],deps:["universal-gravitation","kepler-laws"],
simple:"Galaxies and clusters behave as if they contain about five times more mass than we can see — but nobody knows what it is.",
intuitive:"By Kepler's laws, stars far from a galaxy's centre should orbit slower, as outer planets do. Vera Rubin and Kent Ford (1970s) found the speeds stay flat far out — implying unseen mass surrounding galaxies. Fritz Zwicky (1933) had found galaxies in the Coma cluster moving too fast to be held by visible mass. Gravitational lensing, the CMB fluctuations, and the Bullet Cluster (2006: mass separated from hot gas) all point the same way. Candidates: WIMPs, axions, sterile neutrinos, primordial black holes — none detected despite decades of searches. Alternatives like modified gravity (MOND, 1983) explain some galaxy data but struggle with clusters and the CMB.",
formal:"Flat rotation $v(r) \\approx$ const implies $M(r) = \\dfrac{v^2 r}{G} \\propto r$. Cosmic budget: dark matter ≈ 27%, ordinary matter ≈ 5%.",
philosophy:"Most matter is invisible to us. Is it a new particle, or is our theory of gravity wrong? A case study in how science handles anomalies — like Neptune (new object, success) vs Mercury's orbit (new theory, Einstein).",
why:"Dark matter shaped galaxy formation; identifying it is a top goal of physics.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Particle physics searches, galaxy formation models."},{to:"philosophy",text:"The limits of what we see; anomalies and theory change."},{to:"engineering",text:"Ultra-low-background detectors deep underground."}]});
C({id:"dark-energy",name:"Dark Energy & Accelerating Expansion",type:"open-question",year:1998,era:"frontier",fields:["cosmology","frontiers"],people:["einstein","chandrasekhar"],deps:["expanding-universe","chandrasekhar-limit","big-bang"],
simple:"The universe's expansion is speeding up, driven by something unknown that makes up about 68% of its energy.",
intuitive:"Two teams (Supernova Cosmology Project led by Saul Perlmutter; High-z Supernova Search Team with Brian Schmidt and Adam Riess) measured distant Type Ia supernovae in 1998 and expected to find expansion slowing under gravity. Instead the supernovae were dimmer — farther — than expected: expansion is accelerating. The simplest explanation is Einstein's cosmological constant, an energy of empty space. Quantum field theory's naive estimate of vacuum energy is wrong by up to about 120 orders of magnitude — perhaps the worst prediction in physics. Recent survey results (e.g. DESI, 2024–25) hint that dark energy may evolve with time; this is not settled.",
formal:"Equation of state $w = p/\\rho c^2$; cosmological constant $w = -1$. Acceleration $\\ddot a > 0$ requires $w < -1/3$. $\\Omega_\\Lambda \\approx 0.68$.",
philosophy:"Empty space has energy — and we cannot explain its value. Some invoke anthropic reasoning (Weinberg, 1987) — a controversial idea about what counts as explanation.",
why:"Determines the ultimate fate of the universe. Nobel Prize 2011 (Perlmutter, Schmidt, Riess).",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Cosmology and fundamental physics' biggest puzzle."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Anthropic reasoning and multiverse debates."}]});
C({id:"gravitational-waves",name:"Gravitational Waves (LIGO)",type:"experiment",year:2015,era:"frontier",fields:["general-relativity","astrophysics"],people:["weiss","einstein"],deps:["general-relativity","michelson-morley","black-holes"],demo:"curvature",
simple:"Ripples in spacetime from colliding black holes were detected in 2015 by measuring a length change thousands of times smaller than a proton.",
intuitive:"Einstein predicted (1916) that accelerating masses emit waves of spacetime curvature, but thought them too weak ever to detect. Hulse and Taylor's binary pulsar (discovered 1974) lost energy exactly as gravitational-wave emission predicts (Nobel 1993). LIGO's two 4-km L-shaped laser interferometers (Hanford and Livingston, USA) detected on 14 September 2015 the signal of two black holes (~36 and ~29 solar masses) merging 1.3 billion light-years away: a 'chirp' lasting a fraction of a second, matching relativity's prediction. In 2017 a neutron-star merger was seen in both gravitational waves and light, confirming gravity travels at light speed and revealing gold production.",
formal:"Strain $h = \\Delta L / L \\sim 10^{-21}$, i.e. $\\Delta L \\sim 4\\times10^{-18}$ m over 4 km. Quadrupole radiation: $h \\sim \\dfrac{G}{c^4}\\dfrac{\\ddot Q}{r}$.",
philosophy:"A new sense: astronomy by 'hearing' spacetime instead of seeing light. It vindicated a 100-year-old prediction and a 40-year engineering effort.",
why:"Opened gravitational-wave astronomy; Nobel Prize 2017 (Weiss, Barish, Thorne).",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Seismic isolation, ultra-high vacuum, laser stabilization, quantum squeezing of light."},{to:"science",text:"Black-hole populations, neutron-star physics, origin of heavy elements."}]});
C({id:"bell-theorem",name:"Bell's Theorem & Bell Tests",type:"law",year:1964,era:"frontier",fields:["quantum","quantum-info"],people:["bell"],deps:["entanglement-epr"],
simple:"No theory where particles carry pre-set answers and influences travel no faster than light can match quantum predictions — and experiments side with quantum mechanics.",
intuitive:"Imagine two people answering questions in separate rooms; if they prepared a script beforehand, their answers' correlations obey certain limits. Bell (1964) showed quantum mechanics predicts correlations for entangled particles exceeding such limits. Experiments: Clauser and Freedman (1972), Aspect (1982, with fast switching), and in 2015 'loophole-free' tests in Delft, Vienna and Boulder. Quantum predictions won. Either properties don't exist before measurement, or influences are nonlocal (or more exotic options like superdeterminism).",
formal:"CHSH inequality: for local hidden variables $$|S| = |E(a,b) - E(a,b') + E(a',b) + E(a',b')| \\le 2;$$ quantum mechanics allows up to $2\\sqrt2 \\approx 2.83$ (Tsirelson bound).",
philosophy:"Called 'experimental metaphysics' (Shimony): a philosophical question — is the world locally real? — answered in a lab. Nature is not locally real in Einstein's sense.",
why:"Nobel Prize 2022 (Aspect, Clauser, Zeilinger). Foundation of device-independent quantum cryptography.",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Quantum key distribution, certified random numbers."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Local realism refuted."}]});
C({id:"quantum-computing",name:"Quantum Computing",type:"concept",year:1985,era:"frontier",fields:["quantum-info"],people:["feynman"],deps:["entanglement-epr","stern-gerlach","matrix-mechanics"],math:[["turing-machine","Turing machines"],["p-vs-np","P vs NP"]],
simple:"Computers using qubits — which can be in superpositions and entangled — can solve some problems far faster than ordinary computers.",
intuitive:"Feynman (1981–82) noted that simulating quantum systems on classical computers is exponentially hard, and suggested using quantum systems instead. Deutsch (1985) defined a universal quantum computer. Shor (1994) showed a quantum computer could factor large numbers quickly — threatening RSA encryption. Grover (1996) gave a search speed-up. A qubit is not 'both 0 and 1' in a simple sense; quantum algorithms choreograph interference so wrong answers cancel and right ones reinforce. Current devices (superconducting circuits, trapped ions, neutral atoms, photons) have hundreds to around a thousand physical qubits, but noise remains the central challenge; error correction is advancing.",
formal:"Qubit: $|\\psi\\rangle = \\alpha|0\\rangle + \\beta|1\\rangle$, $|\\alpha|^2 + |\\beta|^2 = 1$. $n$ qubits: state vector of $2^n$ complex amplitudes. Shor's algorithm: polynomial time in number of digits.",
philosophy:"Computation is physical: what is efficiently computable depends on the laws of physics. Deutsch argued this supports many-worlds; most physicists do not draw that conclusion.",
why:"Potential applications in chemistry, materials and cryptography; driving post-quantum cryptography standards (NIST, 2024).",
impact:[{to:"technology",text:"Post-quantum cryptography migration; quantum simulation of molecules; quantum sensing."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Physical limits of computation."}]});
C({id:"higgs-discovery",name:"Discovery of the Higgs Boson",type:"experiment",year:2012,era:"frontier",fields:["particle"],people:["higgs"],deps:["higgs-mechanism","standard-model"],
simple:"In 2012 CERN's Large Hadron Collider found the particle predicted 48 years earlier — the last missing piece of the Standard Model.",
intuitive:"The LHC — a 27-km ring under the France–Switzerland border — collides protons at enormous energies. Higgs bosons are produced very rarely and decay in about 10⁻²² s; physicists find them as small bumps in the statistics of decay products (e.g. two photons, or four leptons) among billions of collisions. On 4 July 2012 the ATLAS and CMS collaborations, each with thousands of scientists, announced a new particle of mass ~125 GeV. Its properties so far match the Standard Model Higgs.",
formal:"$m_H \\approx 125.1$ GeV/c². Discovery threshold: 5σ (chance probability of a fluctuation ~1 in 3.5 million).",
philosophy:"Theory guided by symmetry and consistency predicted a particle decades before machines could find it — and yet the Higgs's light mass raises the 'naturalness' puzzle, since quantum corrections should push it far higher.",
why:"Completed the Standard Model; Nobel Prize 2013 (Englert, Higgs).",
impact:[{to:"engineering",text:"Superconducting magnets, cryogenics, massive data processing (Worldwide LHC Computing Grid)."},{to:"science",text:"Probing the Higgs field and vacuum stability."}]});
C({id:"inflation",name:"Cosmic Inflation",type:"open-question",year:1980,era:"frontier",fields:["cosmology","frontiers"],people:[],deps:["big-bang","cmb","phase-transitions"],
simple:"A proposed burst of extremely rapid expansion in the first tiny fraction of a second, explaining why the universe is so uniform and flat.",
intuitive:"Why does the CMB have the same temperature in regions that could never have exchanged light? Why is space so nearly flat? Alan Guth (1980–81), with refinements by Linde, Albrecht and Steinhardt, and Starobinsky's earlier ideas, proposed that a field drove exponential expansion, stretching a tiny smooth patch to cosmic size. Quantum fluctuations were stretched into the seeds of galaxies, matching the CMB's ripple pattern. But the inflaton field is unknown, predicted primordial gravitational waves have not been detected (a 2014 BICEP2 claim turned out to be dust), and critics (including Steinhardt) question its testability.",
formal:"Scale factor $a(t) \\propto e^{Ht}$ during inflation; at least ~60 'e-folds'. Predicts nearly scale-invariant fluctuations with spectral index $n_s \\approx 0.965$ (measured by Planck).",
philosophy:"Inflation often leads to 'eternal inflation' and a multiverse — raising questions about whether such ideas are testable science.",
why:"The leading theory of the earliest moments, but not yet confirmed.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Origin of cosmic structure; CMB polarization experiments."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Multiverse and testability debates."}]});
C({id:"string-theory",name:"String Theory",type:"open-question",year:1984,era:"frontier",fields:["frontiers","qft"],people:[],deps:["standard-model","general-relativity","gauge-symmetry"],math:[["manifold","Manifolds"],["category","Category theory"]],
simple:"A candidate 'theory of everything' in which particles are tiny vibrating strings — mathematically rich but so far untested.",
intuitive:"Originally (late 1960s) a model of the strong force, string theory was found to include a massless spin-2 particle — the graviton — so it automatically contains gravity. In 1984 Green and Schwarz showed a version was free of fatal inconsistencies (anomalies), launching the 'first superstring revolution'. Different vibration modes would appear as different particles. It requires extra dimensions (10 or 11), presumably curled up very small. Witten (1995) proposed that the five versions are aspects of one 'M-theory'. Maldacena's AdS/CFT correspondence (1997) — gravity in a region equals a quantum field theory on its boundary — is one of its most influential results, used even in condensed-matter physics.",
formal:"String length near the Planck length $\\ell_P = \\sqrt{\\hbar G/c^3} \\approx 1.6\\times10^{-35}$ m. Superstrings: 10 spacetime dimensions; huge 'landscape' of possible vacua (estimates like 10⁵⁰⁰ are often quoted).",
philosophy:"Can a theory be accepted without experimental confirmation? Supporters cite consistency and mathematical fruitfulness; critics (e.g. Smolin, Woit) argue it has become unfalsifiable. The landscape revived anthropic reasoning.",
why:"The most developed approach to quantum gravity; a major source of new mathematics.",
impact:[{to:"science",text:"Black-hole entropy counting, holography (AdS/CFT)."},{to:"philosophy",text:"Testability, beauty and the scientific method."}]});
C({id:"quantum-gravity",name:"Quantum Gravity",type:"open-question",year:2000,era:"frontier",fields:["frontiers","general-relativity","quantum"],people:["hawking","penrose"],deps:["general-relativity","qed","hawking-radiation"],
simple:"Our two best theories — general relativity and quantum mechanics — contradict each other at extreme scales. Uniting them is physics' greatest open problem.",
intuitive:"General relativity treats spacetime as a smooth, dynamic geometry; quantum theory treats everything as quantized and probabilistic, on a fixed background. Usually they don't overlap: gravity matters for big things, quantum for small. But in black holes and the Big Bang both matter. Naively quantizing gravity gives uncontrollable infinities. Approaches: string theory, loop quantum gravity (space made of discrete loops; Ashtekar, Rovelli, Smolin), causal sets, asymptotic safety, emergent gravity from entanglement. Experiments are extremely hard: effects are expected near the Planck energy (~10¹⁹ GeV), about 10¹⁵ times beyond the LHC. Table-top proposals aim to test whether gravity can entangle masses.",
formal:"Planck scales: $\\ell_P \\approx 1.6\\times10^{-35}$ m, $t_P \\approx 5.4\\times10^{-44}$ s, $E_P \\approx 1.2\\times10^{19}$ GeV — where $\\hbar$, $G$ and $c$ all matter.",
philosophy:"Is spacetime fundamental or emergent from something deeper (perhaps quantum information)? What is time in a quantum universe (the 'problem of time')?",
why:"Needed to understand the Big Bang, black-hole interiors, and the information paradox.",
impact:[{to:"philosophy",text:"Nature of space, time and reality."},{to:"science",text:"Black holes, early universe."}]});
